          %title Indefinite integrals
          %h1#indefinite_integrals Indefinite integrals
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions_and_notation"} Definitions and notation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalizations"} Generalizations
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_cartesian_spaces"} In cartesian spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#on_manifolds"} On manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_algebraic_limit_fields"} In algebraic limit fields
              %li
                %a{:href => "#see_also"} See also
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An indefinite integral is something less definite than a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definite+integral"} definite integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            or other concrete quantity, an indefinite integral is typically another variable quantity of the same type as the integrand.
          %p
            The term ‘indefinite integral’ is itself rather indefinite, having been used for a variety of slightly different concepts. Both
            %em semidefinite integrals
            and
            %em antiderivatives
            are more precise versions of indefinite integrals. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+calculus"} fundamental theorem of calculus
            is basically the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            that these different kinds of indefinite integral are essentially the same thing.
          %h2#definitions_and_notation Definitions and notation
          %p
            To begin with, we will discuss the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            will be an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
          #semi.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
              (usually in the domain of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+closure"} closure
              %strong semidefinite integral
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              (or with
              %strong initial point
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi t
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto \int_a^x f(t) \,\mathrm{d}t .
            %p
              (If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^x
              as
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi x
                        %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\int_x^a
          %p
            The semidefinite integral is defined in terms of the definite integral. We can put names such as ‘Riemann’ and ‘Lebesgue’ between ‘semidefinite’ and ‘integral’ to specify a particular kind of definite integral to be used. Note that the domain of the semidefinite integral is an interval containing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and contained in the domain of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            (or at least in its closure if we allow
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                improper integral
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/improper+integral"} ?
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+functions"} almost functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            as a
            %span.newWikiWord
              locally integrable function
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/locally+integrable+function"} ?
            on a closed interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            containing
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            as its domain.
          %p
            The value at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of the semidefinite integral from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            may be denoted
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
          %p
            for short. Notice that this notation has no dummy variable; we only need to introduce the dummy variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            to unfold the definition. (Indeed, some writers will abuse notation, writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi x
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^x f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            for the semidefinite integral.) But as in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}y/\mathrm{d}x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            here is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
            either, since we cannot freely use substitution; it has to be viewed as
            %span.newWikiWord
              variable quantity
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/variable+quantity"} ?
            instead. Rather, if you want to evaluate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is some number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x)|_{x=b}
            following the general logic of variable quantities; but unwrapping the definition of semidefinite integral, this can also be written as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            has now become a dummy variable.)
          #semiIV.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are real numbers (with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              in the domain of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              or its closure), then the
              %strong indefinite integral
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              with
              %strong initial value
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi t
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto C + \int_a^x f(t) \,\mathrm{d}t .
            %p
              We may write this value as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C + \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
              for short.
          %p
            This is only one of the meanings of ‘indefinite integral’, but it is the only one that doesn't have alternative unambiguous terminology. Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the value of the indefinite integral at
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the initial value if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is the initial point. But for authors who use this concept, there is often no need to mention either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (and hence no terminology needed for them), because they are interested only in whether some other function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is an indefinite integral of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a locally integrable function on some closed interval.
          #antider.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a partial function from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is an
              %strong antiderivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              (or an
              %strong antidifferential
              of
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \,\mathrm{d}x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              on its domain:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall\, x \in \dom F,\; f(x) = F'(x) .
            %p
              A posteriori,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              must be
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiable
          %p
            This is the usual meaning of ‘indefinite integral’ in modern Calculus textbooks using the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+integral"} Riemann integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an interval.
          #almostantider.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a Lebesgue-measurable partial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+function"} almost function
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is an
              %strong almost antiderivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is the derivative of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+everywhere"} almost everywhere
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ess
                    %mo ∀
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{ess}\forall\, x \in \dom F,\; f(x) = F'(x) .
            %p
              We are especially interested in the case where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolutely+continuous+function"} absolutely continuous
          %p
            This is not standard terminology, but it fits in well with other ‘almost’ terminology in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The main property linking the different kinds of indefinite integral is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+calculus"} fundamental theorem of calculus
            (FTC). For various definitions of integral, one can prove that every semidefinite integral, or more generally any indefinite integral in the sense of Definition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#semiIV"}
          %p
            Indefinite integrals provide solutions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x} = f(x),\; y|_{x=a} = C
          %p
            is the indefinite integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with initial point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and initial value
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = C + \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x .
          %p Some more general initial-value problems can be solved similarly; for example, the implicit solution to the separable IVP
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(y) \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x} = f(x),\; y|_{x=a} = C
          %p is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi C
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_C g(y) \,\mathrm{d}y = \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x .
          %p Similarly, the solution to the linear IVP
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x} + P(x) \,y = Q(x),\; y|_{x=a} = C
          %p is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi a
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = \frac{C + \int_a Q(x) \,\mu(x) \,\mathrm{d}x}{\mu(x)} ,
          %p
            where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi exp
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(x) = \exp \int_a P(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %h3#in_cartesian_spaces In cartesian spaces
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is an exterior
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+space"} cartesian space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            to be any real-valued function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}f = \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (or perhaps its closure), we can define the value of the semidefinite integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            with initial point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            to be the integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            along a straight line segment from
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %span.newWikiWord
              star-convex set
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/star-convex+set"} ?
            radiating from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and contained in (the closure of)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            on a star-convex set is an indefinite integral. Conversely, every indefinite integral is an antiderivative if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is closed. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is not closed, then it still has indefinite integrals (as long as it's continuous or otherwise locally integrable), but these are no longer antiderivatives (which non-closed forms never have).
          %p
            This can perhaps be generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifolds"} Riemannian manifolds
            by considering integrals along
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesics"} geodesics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is integrable, we can define the indefinite integral this way at every point except the one directly opposite the initial point.)
          %p However, this straight-line definition seems rather artificial, and it might be best to use the more notion of semidefinite integral below, applicable only to closed forms but on more general manifolds.
          %h3#on_manifolds On manifolds
          %p
            We can generalize to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+differential+forms"} exterior differential forms
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifolds"} differentiable manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is an
            %strong exterior antiderivative
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+derivative"} exterior derivative
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is a
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a point in its domain, then the semidefinite integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            with initial point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is defined at another point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            iff the integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is the same along any path from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            (and then that integral is the value). We can define an indefinite integral by adding a constant initial value. Then every antiderivative on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path-connected
            domain is an indefinite integral, and conversely every indefinite integral is an antiderivative. By definition,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is exact iff an antiderivative exists, and therefore iff an indefinite integral exists on each
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                path-connected component
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/path-connected+component"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is closed iff it has an indefinite integral on a neighbourhood of each point; if the domain of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply-connected+space"} simply connected
          %p It remains to consider a good notion of semidefinite and indefinite integrals for higher-rank forms.
          %h3#in_algebraic_limit_fields In algebraic limit fields
          %p
            In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+limit+field"} algebraic limit field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton-Leibniz+operator"} Newton-Leibniz operator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{D}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:S \to F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton-Leibniz+operator"} Newton-Leibniz operator
            at
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{g \in D^1(S, F) \vert \tilde{D}(g) = f\}
          %p
            An
            %strong antiderivative
            is an element of the above subset.
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiable function
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+function"} integrable function
